{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"fields":{"description":"## Context\nADR 0100 makes the immutable route registry and parser factories the authority for command identity, grammar, and committed help. Twenty-one visible intercept routes support `rebar <command> --help` but remain absent from overview help and fail under `rebar help <command>`.\n\n## Plan\n- Derive visible overview membership from the existing route registry.\n- For every registry route that owns top-level help, render one committed route artifact in the existing `gen_cli_help.py` path using the same `_unwrap_usage` and `_collapse_metavars` normalization contract already used by `gen_cli_reference.py`. This makes the artifact byte-stable across Python 3.11, 3.12, and 3.13.\n- Serve both `rebar help <command>` and top-level intercept `rebar <command> --help` from those exact committed bytes; stop the current raw-argparse fallthrough only for routes whose registry metadata says they own top-level help.\n- Regenerate package help and the CLI reference from those same committed route bytes through the existing generators. This fits ADR 0100 because one normalized, registry-owned artifact remains the single source for runtime help and generated reference content.\n- Preserve hidden aliases, retired spellings, nested command help, no-extra startup, and pre-operation no-mount behavior.\n- Add parameterized behavioral coverage over all visible canonical intercept routes returned by the registry, currently twenty-one, plus generator and byte-equality coverage.\n- Do not add another router, parser, or help source.\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n- [ ] Every visible canonical route appears exactly once in overview help.\n- [ ] A parameterized registry-driven test proves `rebar help <command>` succeeds for all visible canonical intercept routes, currently twenty-one, without executing work or mounting a store.\n- [ ] For every route that owns top-level help, `rebar help <command>` and `rebar <command> --help` return the exact bytes of the same committed normalized artifact rather than raw argparse output.\n- [ ] The committed intercept artifacts and help drift checks are byte-stable and pass under the Python 3.11, 3.12, and 3.13 CI matrix.\n- [ ] Hidden aliases and retired spellings remain absent from overview discovery.\n- [ ] Nested command help retains its current ownership.\n- [ ] Help and CLI reference drift checks pass.\n- [ ] No competing routing or parser mechanism is introduced."}},"env_id":"b8a354e0-12c3-4cf2-bbf1-fcc9685d5126","event_type":"EDIT","timestamp":1787418411973702001,"uuid":"36d7640d-a647-441b-8f66-27e4c0866a0f"}